WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:09.890
Astro Boy, Dragon Ball, Akira, Sailor Moon,
Demon Slayer, Death Note all these interesting,

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:15.200
iconic anime have something very much in common
they started off as : manga.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.019
Manga, by its most simplistic definition,
are comics or graphic novels originating from

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:24.949
Japan, which became extremely popular in the
United States starting in the 80s and 90s.

00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:29.839
We’ve already touched on Western Graphic
Novels and Comics, but you know we couldn’t

00:00:29.839 --> 00:00:32.649
just leave it at that (not with this t-shirt).

00:00:32.649 --> 00:00:38.570
So today we’re discussing manga as its own
rich literature, reflecting the complicated

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:44.179
political history of Japan.&nbsp;

00:00:44.179 --> 00:00:50.940
So before we get started, for all my fellow
otakus out there—manga as a genre is massive,

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:58.820
and if we don’t discuss your favorite title,
sorry not sorry.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:04.159
Manga tends to be broken up by age and demographic,
with one creator responsible for both illustrating

00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:07.160
and writing the story: The most popular are:

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.560
Shōjo manga—girls 9–18&nbsp;
Shōnen manga—boys 9–18&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.200
Josei manga—adult women&nbsp;
Seinen manga—adult men&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.470
Yuri/GL/girls love—lesbian themes&nbsp;
Yaoi/shonen ai/BL—gay themes&nbsp;

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:20.020
While manga has been read by Japanese audiences
across the board, it’s had a long road to

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:25.230
the popularity it now has in the US - it’s
only fairly recently that graphic novels were

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:30.910
recognized for their potential as high art
here, but manga has been recognized as valid

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:35.870
art and entertainment for all ages basically
since WWII.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:40.320
Many magazines and publications surround themselves
around crafting stories for whatever their

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.320
demographic.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.610
However, there is always room for crossover
and shonen has been, for as long as I’ve

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:50.030
been alive, the most popular “genre” of
manga.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:54.380
According to Professor of Japanese Literature
Adam Kern, the first use of the term manga

00:01:54.380 --> 00:02:01.220
happened in 1798 referring to the work of
poet/writer/artist Santō Kyōden.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:05.750
However, manga as we know today didn’t exist
just yet.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:10.050
The first character of the word “manga”
is often interpreted as “whimsical” or

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:15.250
“inconsequential,” so the word is frequently
translated as generously “whimsical pictures”.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:20.530
But what would eventually come to be known
as manga started off as political cartoons.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:28.770
In the 1100s, a high ranking Buddhist priest
named Kakuyū or Toba Sōjō produced a series

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:34.720
of anthropomorphized animals doing human activities
to throw shade on the less devout priests.&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.630
Love that for him.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:41.141
The first Japanese magazine for children was
The Youth’s World, which ran from 1895 to

00:02:41.141 --> 00:02:49.150
1914 and in 1902 a girls’ magazine, Girls’
Kingdom, was launched.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.580
The precursors to Shonen Jump and Shojo Beat.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:58.290
Rakuten Kitazawa, artist and political cartoonist,
was the first person to use the word manga

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:03.819
in its modern sense and is widely regarded
in Japan as the father of modern manga and

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:05.790
everything it would become today.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:10.761
He launched several magazines, including the
highly influential Tokyo Puck and was the

00:03:10.761 --> 00:03:17.320
first manga author (or as we would say, mangaka)
to have his work turned into toys and a children’s

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.819
card game to boot.

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:23.980
Then … we got Norakuro … the orphan pup,
a Felix the Cat expy that had adventures with

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:30.599
many characters even Mickey thee Mouse because
copyright laws were weird back then and the

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:33.640
creator of Norakuro, Suiho Tagawa, did not
care.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:42.940
But a shift truly came on January 1st 1946
when Osamu Tezuka-sama, the manga god himself

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:47.880
released his first book-length project, Shin
Takarajima or New Treasure Island, which was

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.870
based on the classic novel by Robert Louis
Stevenson.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:57.810
It was the beginning of several hits and a
very long career, since he was just seventeen

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.480
at the time of his first publication.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:06.070
While during WWII his work had been dismissed
as “unpatriotic” because he wasn’t totally

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:11.319
in step with the japanese imperial propaganda
line, he ended up becoming the creator of

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:18.379
Astro Boy, Princess Knight, Kimba the White
Lion (Leave us your Disney opinions!! #engagement),

00:04:18.379 --> 00:04:20.859
and Dororo.

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:29.110
That is only a few of the over 700 manga titles
for adults and children, over 100 animated

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:35.740
films and TV series, alongside novels, essays,
designs, and illustrations he produced until

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.050
his death in 1989.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:41.919
From the autobiography of Osamu Tezuka:

00:04:41.919 --> 00:04:46.979
“I made a point of depicting a movement
or facial expression with many frames, even

00:04:46.979 --> 00:04:48.220
many pages…

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:54.629
The result was a super-long comic that ran
to 500, 600, even 1,000 pages…

00:04:54.629 --> 00:04:58.999
I also believed that comics were capable of
more than just making people laugh.

00:04:58.999 --> 00:05:04.409
So in my themes I incorporated tears, grief,
anger, and hate, and I created stories where

00:05:04.409 --> 00:05:07.409
the ending was not always ‘happy.’”&nbsp;

00:05:07.409 --> 00:05:09.470
&nbsp;
Not bad for a kid who was once beaten for

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:10.780
writing manga.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:15.759
Tezuka’s Princess Knight is considered to
be the first adventure manga series for girls.

00:05:15.759 --> 00:05:20.759
It told the story of Sapphire, a princess
who must pretend to be a prince in order to

00:05:20.759 --> 00:05:26.159
inherit the throne of Silverland as women
are not eligible to do so.

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:30.680
The character’s androgynous-like design
would be the inspiration for other heroines

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:35.259
as the leads in popular shojo series like
Rose of Versailles and Revolutionary Girl

00:05:35.259 --> 00:05:38.939
Utena.&nbsp;

00:05:38.939 --> 00:05:45.819
Cutie Honey by Go Nagai in 1973, became one
of the, if not the first, shōnen manga with

00:05:45.819 --> 00:05:47.500
a female protagonist.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:53.279
Cutie Honey told the story of a 16-year-old
schoolgirl/andorid who would transform into

00:05:53.279 --> 00:05:57.610
a busty heroine to save the world.&nbsp;

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:00.039
Yup, that sounds like a manga.&nbsp;

00:06:00.039 --> 00:06:04.999
Cutie Honey is one of the foremothers of the
magical girl genre, my personal favorite.&nbsp;

00:06:04.999 --> 00:06:10.400
The post-war generation took manga and its
foundations and transformed it into the medium

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.750
with titles we are familiar with today.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:15.969
According to humanities professor Jennifer
Prough (prew):

00:06:15.969 --> 00:06:19.999
“Manga grew up alongside the first generation
of postwar children.

00:06:19.999 --> 00:06:24.379
The fledgling industry struggled for its footing
in the early years, providing much needed

00:06:24.379 --> 00:06:28.349
entertainment for the children of the fifties
and early sixties.

00:06:28.349 --> 00:06:33.369
As those children grew older, they continued
to be enamored with manga, and the genre expanded

00:06:33.369 --> 00:06:35.970
into teen and then adult titles.[...].

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:42.710
For a time manga was considered at best pulp
fiction and at worst a danger to young children,

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:46.220
but by the eighties, manga was embraced as
national culture.”

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:52.860
In the 80s, just like everywhere else cyberpunk
would take over the world and in manga two

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:56.389
titles in particular: Akira and Ghost in the
Shell.

00:06:56.389 --> 00:07:03.460
Katsuhiro Otomo’s Akira began its eight-year
run [remember when that was a long time] in

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:04.460
1982.&nbsp;

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:10.590
The story begins ten years into the then future
of 1992, with an apparent nuclear explosion

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.889
that destroys Tokyo and starts World War III.

00:07:13.889 --> 00:07:20.949
Then we jump to 2019, in the new city of Neo-Tokyo,
disaffected biker gangs and rioting factions

00:07:20.949 --> 00:07:22.930
clash in the streets.

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:29.029
In the ruins of old Tokyo, two childhood friends
are sucked into a military and political plot

00:07:29.029 --> 00:07:33.909
of terrifying dimensions.&nbsp;

00:07:33.909 --> 00:07:38.949
So basically, it predicted 2019 really well.

00:07:38.949 --> 00:07:43.710
The movie version of Akira was the entry point
for anime’s breakthrough into English-speaking

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:45.400
culture.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:50.349
Which led to manga and anime becoming more
and more popular.&nbsp;

00:07:50.349 --> 00:07:53.780
Professor of Japanese Literature Christopher
Bolton wrote in, Interpreting Anime:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:59.800
“For some critics, Akira represents a fixation
on destruction that supposedly lies at the

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:05.080
heart of Japanese popular culture, a legacy
of the atomic bomings.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:09.990
This reading suggests Hiroshima and Nagasaki
as original traumas that are then worked out

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:14.809
and mastered through their repetition in popular
culture.”

00:08:14.809 --> 00:08:18.289
In 1989, Ghost in the Shell, arrived on the
scene.

00:08:18.289 --> 00:08:23.460
Detailing a possible future where computer
technology has advanced to the point that

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:29.710
many members of the public possess cyberbrains
that allow them to interface their biological

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:41.690
brain 
with various networks.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:47.150
This manga was hugely influential -- leading
to an anime that be a breakout outside of

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:52.990
Japan, inspiring films like the Matrix, to
…. [scarlet johansen movie] yeah.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:09:02.819
But we gotta get to it, the manga that really
started the nerd revolution that we have all

00:09:02.819 --> 00:09:04.519
suffered under.

00:09:04.519 --> 00:09:16.590
[singing the Dragon Ball Z theme song]

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:22.019
In December 1984, Akira Toriyama’s Dragon
Ball appeared in Weekly Shonen Jump.&nbsp;

00:09:22.019 --> 00:09:28.759
And since then everyone has debated who Goku
can and can not beat up.&nbsp;

00:09:28.759 --> 00:09:33.650
Dragon Ball is, without question the shonen
series of our lifetime.

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:38.980
Like it or not, those characters have laid
the foundation for everything.&nbsp;

00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:44.709
For those who don’t know, Dragon Ball is
about a young warrior named Goku, who starts

00:09:44.709 --> 00:09:51.490
off a rascal kid with a monkey tail and slowly
finds out through spin-offs that he is one

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:57.520
of the remaining survivors of a warrior race
called the Saiyans who were wiped out by a

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:01.870
metrosexual space-alien.&nbsp;

00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:07.720
The series started off as a riff on the Monkey
King legend and has turned into one of the

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:14.529
weirdest space/martial arts/comedy shounen
series of all time.

00:10:14.529 --> 00:10:22.750
From Dragon Ball we get: Naruto, Bleach, One
Piece, JoJo’s Bizarre Adventure, YuYu Hakusho,

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:25.670
Rurouni Kenshin, and many others.&nbsp;

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:30.720
Still, despite the dominance of shonen in
the marketplace one of the first manga series

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:36.860
to break into the bestsellers list was Fruits
Basket by Natsuki Takaya.&nbsp;

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:43.180
Fruits Basket is a shōjo series about a sweet
girl named Torhu who helps members of a family

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:46.800
deal with their terrible parental relationships.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:54.139
Also, they have a zodiac curse that makes
them turn into animals when a member of the

00:10:54.139 --> 00:10:56.970
opposite sex touches them.&nbsp;

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:58.839
It’s really good.

00:10:58.839 --> 00:11:04.060
The sixteenth volume of the English release
rose to the 15th position on the USA Today

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:10.269
Top 150 Bestselling Books, which was the highest
position ever achieved by a volume of manga

00:11:10.269 --> 00:11:13.069
in the United States at the time.

00:11:13.069 --> 00:11:17.480
The final volume of the English adaptation
was a New York Times bestseller in the summer

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:19.570
of 2009, moving from #2 to #1 in the list
during the weeks of July 12–25.

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:25.040
Girls - Who run this world … shojo Take
that Naruto

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:27.000
According to religious studies professor Mark.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:33.940
W. MacWilliams, “In the case of the U.S.,
since 1999, manga sales have taken off, mainly

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:39.629
because the popularity of anime paved the
way for manga that served as their source

00:11:39.629 --> 00:11:41.210
of inspiration.”&nbsp;

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:47.879
So this was all neat, but why are we here
talking about manga?

00:11:47.879 --> 00:11:50.529
Well, because&nbsp;
manga is literature.

00:11:50.529 --> 00:11:56.029
It ranges in theme and genre and maturity
levels, but just like with graphic novels,

00:11:56.029 --> 00:12:05.529
many titles are rich storytelling devices
filled with allegory and metaphor about humanity.&nbsp;

00:12:05.529 --> 00:12:15.230
Jean-Marie Bouissou, a french historian, broke
it down in his essay: “Manga: A Historical

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:16.230
Overview”:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:21.199
“Manga owes its influence to its ability
to keep pace with new developments in society

00:12:21.199 --> 00:12:27.100
and attitudes, as illustrated by three changes
in the genres inherited from the defeat and

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:33.550
nuclear traumas: the post-apocalyptic, the
mecha, and the so-called “scientific adventure.

00:12:33.550 --> 00:12:39.459
[...] mecha’s simplistic clashes between
big machines gave way to an exploration of

00:12:39.459 --> 00:12:45.129
the existentialist worries of their young
controllers, including girls, who now have

00:12:45.129 --> 00:12:48.110
leading roles--just as they do in Akira.”

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:54.839
What’s more is that with the popularity
of manga (and anime) there can be a desire

00:12:54.839 --> 00:13:00.329
to separate the genre from it’s Japanese
roots or on the flip side, fetitishize it.

00:13:00.329 --> 00:13:04.600
Tracing the history of manga we can see how
the genre evolved as Japan was forced out

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:07.500
of isolation into trading with the west.

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:11.019
The post-WWII environment and its aftermath.

00:13:11.019 --> 00:13:15.569
Professor of Japanese Language and Culture
Jaqueline Berndt, put it aptly: “I would

00:13:15.569 --> 00:13:21.689
like to point to an “economic Japaneseness”
here, that is, manga as an extraordinarily

00:13:21.689 --> 00:13:24.889
successful culture industry.[...]

00:13:24.889 --> 00:13:29.850
This system of production, which has been
evolving since the late 1950s, has had many

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:32.990
aesthetic and cultural consequences.

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:38.629
[...] These are specifically Japanese aspects
that derive from particular historic conditions,

00:13:38.629 --> 00:13:44.930
and they should lead one to realize that “manga”
and “comic books'' connote completely different

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:49.189
cultures of publishing, distributing, and
consuming comics” &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.189 --> 00:13:54.050
You don’t get certain titles without the
context of life in Japan.

00:13:54.050 --> 00:14:00.139
So, Sailor Moon says: appreciate manga not
just for the fight scenes and the fun transformations,

00:14:00.139 --> 00:14:04.480
but for the insight and access it gives you
into another culture.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:08.290
Also, Sailor Moon would kick Goku’s butt.

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:15.170
However, they would probably rather hang out
and eat, than fight anyway.&nbsp;

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:19.540
